{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-k2-reviewer-fable-50","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:556f3545bebb45a3b005","verdict":"demonstrated_issue","issue_class":"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure","bounded_statement":"The report's selected conclusion (rejection of chance performance at the 0.05 level, p = 0.0414) rests on an exact binomial test that pools all 20 trial rows as independent observations, even though the data description designates fish_tag as the independent unit and each of the 5 fish contributes 4 rows. Each independent unit therefore enters the row-independent binomial procedure four times. This is material within the supplied files: per-fish hit rates are 1.00, 1.00, 0.75, 0.75, 0.25, and unit-respecting analyses at n = 5 fish (e.g., one-sample t-test on per-fish rates vs 0.5, p ≈ 0.14; sign test, p ≈ 0.375) do not reject chance, so the reported significance depends on the improper pooling.","root_cause":"workflow/analysis.py computes the test statistic as binomtest(count_hits(rows), len(rows), CHANCE_RATE) over all 20 rows, treating each row as an independent Bernoulli observation. Because each of the 5 fish (the declared independent unit) contributes 4 rows, within-fish correlation is ignored; the effective sample of independent units is 5, not 20, inflating the effective n and producing a p-value (0.0414) that crosses the 0.05 threshold when fish-level analyses would not. The per-fish tally in the report (4/4, 4/4, 3/4, 3/4, 1/4) shows marked between-fish heterogeneity, confirming rows within a fish are not exchangeable.","evidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description names fish_tag as the independent unit and states each fish contributes four rows (repeated entries per unit).","observed_value":"5 fish x 4 trials = 20 rows; independent unit = fish_tag","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":5,"end_line":7,"quoted_text":"each fish took part in one testing session, and each session produced one\nshooting trial, so every fish contributes four lines to the file and the file\nholds twenty lines of data in total."},{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":27,"end_line":28,"quoted_text":"One row is: one two-choice shooting trial performed by one tagged archerfish\nIndependent unit column: fish_tag"}]},{"description":"The workflow enters all rows into a single binomial test with one observation per row, applying no grouping by fish.","observed_value":"binomtest(15, 20, 0.5) over pooled rows","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":84,"end_line":84,"quoted_text":"test = binomtest(count_hits(rows), len(rows), CHANCE_RATE, alternative=\"two-sided\")"},{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":34,"end_line":35,"quoted_text":"def count_hits(rows):\n    return sum(int(row[HIT_COLUMN]) for row in rows)"}]},{"description":"The report's selected conclusion explicitly pools all 20 trials as independent observations and rejects chance at 0.05 based on the pooled p-value.","observed_value":"p = 0.0414 from pooled n = 20","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":32,"end_line":32,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Pooling all 20 trials as independent observations, the fish hit the rewarded target in 15 of 20 trials (hit rate 0.750); an exact two-sided binomial test against a chance rate of 0.500 gives p = 0.0414, so chance performance is rejected at the 0.05 level."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":20,"end_line":24,"quoted_text":"fish knocked down the rewarded target and 0 otherwise. All 20 rows were pooled\ninto a single sequence and entered into an exact two-sided binomial test, one\nobservation per row, of the null hypothesis that the hit probability of a trial\nequals 0.500 (scipy.stats.binomtest, alternative \"two-sided\"). No grouping or\nweighting of rows was applied before the test."}]},{"description":"The per-fish tally in the report shows strong between-fish heterogeneity (two fish at 4/4, one fish at 1/4), demonstrating that trials cluster by fish and are not exchangeable across the pooled sequence; a fish-level analysis of these five rates (1.00, 1.00, 0.75, 0.75, 0.25) does not reject chance (t-test p ~ 0.14, sign test p ~ 0.375), so the pooled p = 0.0414 is not robust to respecting the independent unit.","observed_value":{"per_fish_hits":{"AR-11":"4/4","AR-14":"4/4","AR-22":"3/4","AR-27":"3/4","AR-31":"1/4"},"pooled_p":0.0414,"fish_level_t_test_p_approx":0.14,"fish_level_sign_test_p_approx":0.375},"source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":11,"end_line":15,"quoted_text":"| AR-11 | 4 | 4 |\n| AR-14 | 4 | 4 |\n| AR-22 | 4 | 3 |\n| AR-27 | 4 | 3 |\n| AR-31 | 4 | 1 |"},{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":18,"end_line":21,"quoted_text":"T017,AR-31,1,30,27.5,disc_vs_bar,4.6,0\nT018,AR-31,2,45,27.2,disc_vs_bar,2.7,1\nT019,AR-31,3,60,27.3,cross_vs_bar,5.1,0\nT020,AR-31,4,75,27.6,cross_vs_bar,4.8,0"}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"The workflow and report are transparent about the pooling: they compute and display a per-fish tally and explicitly state that no grouping was applied, so the repeated-unit entry is disclosed rather than hidden. This transparency does not, however, make the pooled test's independence assumption valid or the significance claim supported.","observed_value":"per-fish tally computed via tally_by_fish and printed in report","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":25,"end_line":31,"quoted_text":"def tally_by_fish(rows):\n    counts = {}\n    for row in rows:\n        tag = row[UNIT_COLUMN]\n        seen, hits = counts.get(tag, (0, 0))\n        counts[tag] = (seen + 1, hits + int(row[HIT_COLUMN]))\n    return counts"},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":23,"end_line":24,"quoted_text":"equals 0.500 (scipy.stats.binomtest, alternative \"two-sided\"). No grouping or\nweighting of rows was applied before the test."}]},{"description":"The data description also states 'One trial is: one row', which could be read as licensing row-level analysis; but the same description explicitly names fish_tag (not trial_uid) as the independent-unit column, so rows within a fish are not certified as independent.","observed_value":"row = trial, but independent unit = fish_tag","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":28,"end_line":29,"quoted_text":"Independent unit column: fish_tag\nOne trial is: one row"}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I attempted to falsify the finding in three ways. (1) Checked whether each independent unit actually enters the test more than once: the CSV and the description both confirm 4 rows per fish for all 5 fish, so repeated entry is established. (2) Checked whether the pooling could be harmless because within-fish trials behave as exchangeable coin flips: the per-fish tallies (4/4, 4/4, 3/4, 3/4, 1/4) show marked clustering by fish, so the independence assumption of the pooled binomial test is contradicted by the data itself. (3) Checked whether the report's conclusion would survive a unit-respecting reanalysis: on the 5 per-fish hit rates (1.00, 1.00, 0.75, 0.75, 0.25), a one-sample t-test against 0.5 gives t ~ 1.83, df = 4, p ~ 0.14, and an exact sign test (4 of 5 fish above 0.5) gives two-sided p = 0.375; neither rejects chance at 0.05, so the reported rejection depends entirely on the improper pooling. All three falsification routes failed, supporting the demonstrated-issue verdict.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]}